 {
  "content": {
   "text": "### How TCP Congestion Control Works\n\n",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": "TCP congestion control prevents senders from overwhelming network routers and links, balancing high throughput with fairness and stability. Unlike flow control,",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " which protects the receiver's buffer, congestion control responds to capacity limits inside the network itself.\n\nA sender maintains a",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " **congestion window (`cwnd`)**, limiting how many unacknowledged bytes can be in flight at once. The effective",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " sending limit is the minimum of `cwnd` and the receiver's advertised window. Because network capacity is unknown at",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " the start and changes dynamically, TCP continuously probes for bandwidth and backs off when packet loss or delay signals congestion.\n\nThe classic algorithm operates through four core phases",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": ":\n\n* **Slow Start:** A new connection begins conservatively with a small `cwnd`. For every acknowledgment (ACK) received, `cwnd",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": "` increases by one maximum segment size (MSS), doubling roughly every round-trip time (RTT). This exponential growth quickly discovers",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " available capacity until `cwnd` reaches the slow-start threshold (`ssthresh`).\n* **Congestion Avoidance",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": ":** Once `cwnd` exceeds `ssthresh`, growth shifts from exponential to linear, adding roughly one MSS per RTT. This cautious",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " probing explores extra bandwidth without abruptly saturating bottlenecks.\n* **Fast Retransmit:** Rather than waiting for a costly",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " retransmission timeout, TCP treats three duplicate ACKs as evidence that a segment was lost while subsequent packets still arrived. The sender immediately retransmits the missing segment",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": ".\n* **Fast Recovery:** After fast retransmit, TCP halves `ssthresh` and adjusts `cwnd",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": "` to match, avoiding a reset to slow start so traffic continues flowing smoothly. If a full timeout occurs instead, TCP",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " assumes severe congestion, sets `ssthresh` to half of `cwnd`, and drops `cwnd` back to one MSS.\n\nModern congestion",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": " control variants refine these baseline rules. **CUBIC** uses a cubic growth function to scale efficiently across fast, long-",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 },
 {
  "content": {
   "text": "distance networks, while **BBR** estimates bottleneck bandwidth and minimum RTT directly to avoid filling router queues and causing excessive queueing delay.",
   "type": "text"
  },
  "sessionUpdate": "agent_message_chunk"
 }
